    "details": {
      "alignment": "neutral",
      "type": {
        "value": "humanoid",
        "subtype": "koopa"
      },
      "cr": 1,
      "biography": {
        "value": "<h1>Koopa Troopa — The Backbone Nobody Thanks</h1><p>The Koopa Troopa is the standard infantry soldier of the Koopa Troop, the bipedal turtle-people who have served as Bowser's primary ground forces for as long as anyone can remember. They walk patrol routes. They man fortress positions. They stand between Mario and whatever objective Mario is stomping toward. And they die—in enormous numbers, with depressing regularity, respawning through mechanisms that nobody fully understands and that the koopas themselves describe only as \"it's a turtle thing.\"</p><p>The average Koopa Troopa is not stupid, despite what the legends suggest. The average Koopa Troopa is a conscript or career soldier who signed on with the Troop because Darkland's economy offers limited alternatives and because Bowser's military provides food, shelter, and a shell-maintenance stipend that civilian life cannot match. They follow orders because the chain of command works, because the alternative is court martial, and because most of them genuinely believe in the Koopa Troop's mission even if they can't quite articulate what that mission is now that Bowser has been absent for weeks and Kamek is issuing orders that don't match what Bowser would have wanted.</p><p>Kamek's return to the Valley of Bowser on Highsun 18 has created a command crisis that the rank-and-file Koopa Troopas are navigating with the specific anxiety of soldiers who know something is wrong but don't have enough information to know what. Kamek is giving orders. The orders are legal under Troop command structure. But the orders don't feel right—they're focused on consolidation and control rather than rescue, on securing Kamek's position rather than finding Bowser, on preparing for something that Kamek hasn't explained. The Troopas follow because following is what they do. Some of them are starting to wonder whether what they do and what they should do have diverged.</p>",
        "public": "A bipedal turtle in standard Koopa Troop equipment, walking a patrol route with the resigned competence of someone who has been doing this job long enough to know it's thankless but not long enough to quit."
      },
